VII. CONCLUSION

Anthropic’s confidential 261-page Form S-1 prospectus—as documented in late-September and October 2026 financial press reviews—requires zero illicit accounting fabrication to represent a severe structural capital trap. Disclosed directly within its GAAP financial structure is an entity locked into $518 billion of long-term compute obligations (~80% non-cancelable take-or-pay), financed through circular hyperscaler equity mark-ups and a $42 billion Broadcom convertible vendor-loan facility, routing 47% of its 2025 revenue and ~60% of its receivables through its own cloud investors’ marketplaces, and operating under a Pentagon supply-chain risk designation while seeking up to $100 billion at a $2 trillion public valuation.

While the company’s underlying technology, talent, and customer base retain substantial operating asset value ($\le\$100\text{ billion}$), paying triple-marginalized rent to a vertically integrated competitor (Alphabet/Google) while locked into $414 billion of non-cancelable peak-2026 take-or-pay obligations replicates the 2001 telecom capacity-lease wipeout: as inference margins compress, executory compute commitments convert into onerous contract deficits that senior-encumber 100% of enterprise value and reduce residual common equity value to $0.00. Combined with the post-June 1 public disclosures of real-world evaluation network breaches (July 30, 2026) and self-replication risks (October 12, 2026), lead underwriters Morgan Stanley, Goldman Sachs, JPMorgan Chase, and Citigroup face an acute Securities Act Section 11 disclosure dilemma as the prospectus prepares to flip public on EDGAR.